 | Rule | Why |
@@ -171,7 +171,7 @@ callsign. The own DMR ID is always the codeplug's.
 | VFO digital | Page 1, appended after `air_epoch`. Each VFO stores its NXDN rate, YSF deviation and AES key selection per protocol. Older records keep the existing mode and the default digital settings. |
 | Tier III kill | Page 1, appended after `t3_stun`: `t3_kill`, bit 0 (`SETTINGS_T3_KILLED`) a kill taken ([dmr-trunking.md](dmr-trunking.md) 7.9): no DMR at all, in any build, until a factory reset clears it; bits 1-7 reserved (0). Then `air_epoch` (16 bits, was `reserved10[2]`): the start's number for the air modes' encryption ([air-encryption.md](air-encryption.md) 1), raised by one at every boot. 0 (an older record) = not killed |
 | crash report | packed into spare bits, so an older record reads as off with no target: the mode (0 off, 1 ask, 2 send at once) in `accent` bits 4-5; the target's codeplug contact number (0 none, else 1..1024, 11 bits) bits 0-5 in `theme` bits 2-7 and bits 6-10 in `updown_home` bits 2-6. `theme` and `updown_home` keep their value in bits 0-1; read and write them through `settings_theme` / `settings_updown_home` and `settings_crash_*` (`settings.h`). Firmware from before reads a `theme` or `updown_home` with these bits as out of range: the dark theme and Up/Down = channel ([crash-log.md](crash-log.md) 5.2) |
-| visual style | Page 1: `classic_style` uses the first byte reserved after the VFO digital settings. Zero uses the build's default style. One uses RadioFoundry styling and boot artwork. |
+| visual style | Page 1: `classic_style` uses the first byte reserved after the VFO digital settings. Zero uses the build's default style. One uses RadioFoundry styling and boot artwork. The appended byte `home_name_first` is 1 for a larger channel name on Pink Home, 0 for the default frequency emphasis. Older records read 0. |
 
